<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solymi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solymi were an ancient people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, placed by Greek writers in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a> and Lycia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> names them as foes of the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a>, and later tradition linked them to the city of Termessos, whose people called themselves Solymians. Their language and origins are unknown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Solymi, also called Solymians, are counted among the earliest known peoples of the lands later called Lycia, Milyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a>, and classical historians took them for the original inhabitants of the southern coast. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> they held the country later called Lycia until the Termilae, ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a>, arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, drove them out and pushed them inland into the mountains of Milyas. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> the king of Lycia sends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a> to fight the glorious Solymi, fierce warriors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> named the native people of Pisidia as Solymi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their heartland came to centre on Mount Solymos, the modern Güllük Dağı in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and accounts make them the founders of Termessos, a strongly defended city more than 1000 metres up its south-western slopes that held out even against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Strabo recorded Solymian as one of four languages spoken in the Kibyran tetrapolis, beside Pisidian, Greek and Lydian; traces of it survived in local personal names and religious inscriptions.</p><p>Their chief god was the mountain deity Solymeus, who was later joined to the Greek pantheon as Zeus Solymeus. Temples and inscriptions to Zeus Solymeus are found throughout the ruins of Termessos, and Roman funerary inscriptions in its necropolis order fines of 1000 drakhmai for violating a tomb to be paid to his temple treasury.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Solymi did not end in a single defeat but were gradually absorbed. After the campaigns of Alexander Greek culture spread strongly through the region, and they took up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a>, civic institutions and customs. Termessos remained powerful under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and in 71 BCE won a formal alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Lex Antonia de Termessibus, which guaranteed its independence and laws. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the Solymian identity faded into provincial culture, and by the third century CE the language and the separate identity had gone. Termessos itself was finally abandoned after an earthquake broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> that supplied it with water.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solymoi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Termessos was built at over 1,000 metres on a mountain north-west of modern Antalya, traditionally by the <b>Solymi</b>, an Anatolian people said to have fought the hero Bellerophon.</blockquote><a href="/state/termessos">Termessos</a></li></ul></section>
